From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Gino D." <25200363+zinosaure@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 08:35:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] gameflex 0097: how many suspend points a game runs ahead is a
 per-game setting (P4, Clair Obscur)

A suspend point (sceAgcSuspendPoint) returns once the GPU has executed everything submitted before the
previous one. Clair Obscur calls 7 points an image, 6 of them in its first millisecond: the 7th closes the
frame, and the first point of the next frame waits for the GPU to finish all of it. An image is then the
command thread's work (36.8 ms) followed by the GPU's tail (13.3 ms), the thread idle meanwhile. 0096 (two
points ahead for every game, not delivered) bought one seventh of an image there, and Astro Bot, one point an
image, no longer started.

- KYTY_SUSPEND_POINTS_AHEAD (1 to 16) sets the credits, read when the GPU thread starts: 1 by default, the
  behaviour of 0095 for every game; a game's patch raises it (Clair Obscur: 8, a whole frame). A call returns
  once the GPU has executed everything before the point that many calls back; the first ones return at once.
  Each point gives its credit back as before, on the priority thread, in tick order.
- More than one point ahead, the frames stay in order across queues (0096's epochs): every submission takes,
  at admission and under the queue lock, the number of points admitted before it, and of the fronts that can
  run the oldest epoch goes first. A frame ahead, the round-robin would record the next frame's compute lists
  before this frame's graphics lists on the one Vulkan queue, and they may rewrite what those read. A front
  that waits lets a later epoch run. At one point ahead the round-robin is unchanged: the next frame cannot
  enter before the GPU has executed what precedes the last point.
- The perf log counts the game's calls, those that found no credit, and their wait (0096).
- GpuSuspendPoint follows the setting: the first SuspendPointsAhead() calls return at once, the next waits
  for the first one's native completion; above one, a compute list admitted after a point runs after the
  graphics list admitted before it.

What the game can observe above one: returning from a suspend point no longer means that the GPU has
executed what was submitted before the previous point. A game that recycles that memory on that guarantee
alone would read or draw over it: the setting is for games whose images were checked with it.

The recompiler and the pipeline code are left alone: the shader store keeps its stamp.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
